gm-method (a 'take every bounded-packing/spatial-indexing speedup' request against a mature engine): PROFILE THE LIVE FRAME FIRST -- most imagined packing/index wins are phantom because the hot paths are usually already bounded and the frame is often GPU-bound. A real live profile (browser `profile` prefix, driven with movement so streaming/update/raycast all run) ranked the steady frame as (program) GPU + texture/shader first-use + terrain texture streaming on top; EVERY candidate JS alloc/spatial node (per-frame .filter/.map, per-chunk Array.push, spatial queries) sat <0.4% -- already bounded (quantized octree, grid broadphase, pooled snapshot interp, O(1) GPU-patch height, BVH view-cache). The ONLY reachable CPU packing win was a low-frequency field (climate biome fractal) re-evaluated per placement CELL. THE GENERALIZABLE WIN: a low-frequency field sampled per fine cell is oversampling -> wrap it in a deterministic SECTOR cache (sample once per coarse sector at its CENTRE, reuse for all cells), which collapses NxN cells to one eval. PARITY for a client/server-shared placement: key the cache by an integer floor of the local coord and sample at the sector centre so BOTH runtimes compute the same key+centre -> byte-identical output (witness with two independent wraps producing 0 mismatches over a patch). HONESTY: when the frame is GPU-bound, say plainly that even a perfect CPU win won't move steady FPS (it helps CPU-bound/weak devices + streaming hitches), and DON'T churn code for sub-threshold/phantom wins -- record them as measured-not-worth. Surface the GPU-bound reality + the one real CPU win to the user as a decision rather than silently shipping marginal packing.